(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #112 — a thing-returning function already handed the
 caller back its OWN destination storage when it fell off the end (plan
 310 s5, predating this fix), so there was no wild pointer to
 dereference; but nothing had ever WRITTEN that storage, so it read
 whatever bytes were already on the caller's stack rather than the
 all-defaults instance an unwritten thing gets everywhere else
 (`generate_thing_decl`, a `.bss` global). The fixed epilogue writes the
 same field-by-field defaults through the destination pointer, so a
 fallen-off point reads its declared defaults (5, 7), not the caller's
 leftover stack.)

A thing called point has
  a number called x is 5,
  a number called y is 7.

To 'maybe' with a boolean called 'the choice'.
    a point called p.
    Set p's x to 99.
    Set p's y to 99.
    If 'the choice' then,
        Return a point, p.

